00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.510
and watch some of the content

00:01:27.510 --> 00:01:29.130
and I'm really excited to introduce them

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.770
and I'm really excited they're here,

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:32.190
and they took a train this morning,

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:34.270
which there's only one
train track in North Dakota

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.240
and then we'll go into all that.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.470
But why don't you introduce
yourselves to the VaynerNation?

00:01:41.290 --> 00:01:42.260
- I'm Jocelyne.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:43.998
- I was gonna say, can
you tell us apart, but?

00:01:43.998 --> 00:01:45.020
(laughing)

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:45.853
- [Gary] I can.

00:01:45.853 --> 00:01:46.940
- But you gave it away.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.000
- I'm Jocelyne.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:48.870
- And I'm Monique Lamoureux.

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:49.937
We're the Lamoureux twins

00:01:49.937 --> 00:01:53.040
and we play hockey for
the U.S. women's team,

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:54.680
but we're currently on maternity leave,

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.440
so we're going to camp in a few weeks,

00:01:56.440 --> 00:01:59.000
tryin' to get back on
the national team, so.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:01.199
- And so, right to the origin story,

00:02:01.199 --> 00:02:05.540
how do these twins from,
and you were born in?

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:06.640
- [Monique] North Dakota.

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.010
- How do these twins
from North Dakota get to

00:02:09.010 --> 00:02:10.520
a place where they get to represent

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:14.090
our country at the highest
levels in their sport?

00:02:14.090 --> 00:02:17.850
- Well we grew up, so our
dad played Division I hockey,

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:19.610
at the University of North Dakota.

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:20.930
We have four older brothers that

00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:24.160
all played hockey and then
we just kind of followed them

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.229
around when we were younger.

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:27.180
- [Gary] And you were the youngest?

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.960
- Yes, we were the youngest.
- Yeah, so we're 30

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:31.350
and then well there's one
more birthday this summer.

00:02:31.350 --> 00:02:35.146
Right now it's 30, 31,
32, 33, 34, our ages.

00:02:35.146 --> 00:02:36.130
- [Gary] Your parents were busy.

00:02:36.130 --> 00:02:37.278
- Yes, they're crazy.

00:02:37.278 --> 00:02:38.111
- [Gary] Clearly.

00:02:38.111 --> 00:02:39.920
- Irresponsible.

00:02:39.920 --> 00:02:42.237
- So, we would just follow them around,

00:02:42.237 --> 00:02:44.290
playing whatever sports they were playing,

00:02:44.290 --> 00:02:45.520
but hockey is the big thing.

00:02:45.520 --> 00:02:46.750
We have long winters there

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:49.980
and so we'd literally, play
outside on the frozen pond

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.660
by our parents house and
we grew up being able to

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:56.160
compete at a high level,
went to a boarding school

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:58.400
right outside Minneapolis.

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:01.170
- And at the earliest
levels you were playing,

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.330
obviously mixed with boys
and things of that nature?

00:03:03.330 --> 00:03:04.810
- With all boys, we actually didn't have

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:05.980
a girls team to play on

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.840
and so yeah there just
weren't enough girls playing,

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:11.840
so we played with boys until high school.

00:03:11.840 --> 00:03:13.670
Yep, and then we went
to a boarding school.

00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:15.430
- I need one or two great stories on this,

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:17.541
'cause, obviously boys
are idiots and probably--

00:03:17.541 --> 00:03:18.374
- [Sisters] Yep.

00:03:18.374 --> 00:03:20.400
- And probably grossly underestimated you

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:21.590
so I'm sure there's gotta be,

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:25.230
I need one classic story
that you guys love to tell.

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.540
- Our dad always told,
so we played three years

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.638
of checking and so when
we started checking

00:03:30.638 --> 00:03:33.360
we were probably middle of pack size-wise

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:35.010
before all the boys
started hitting puberty

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:37.360
and our dad always told us
in practice or in a game,

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.197
he would say, "Find the the
biggest kid on the other team

00:03:40.197 --> 00:03:42.280
"and you put 'em on their ass."

00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:43.967
That's what he would tell us.

00:03:43.967 --> 00:03:45.660
- [Gary] And you listened blindly?

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:46.493
- Yep.

00:03:46.493 --> 00:03:49.740
- Oh yeah, we listened and
then I specifically remember,

00:03:49.740 --> 00:03:52.513
it was our peewee year
and I played defense

00:03:52.513 --> 00:03:55.488
and so I had knocked
a kid over pretty good

00:03:55.488 --> 00:03:57.637
and our coach came back, and he goes,

00:03:57.637 --> 00:04:00.940
"Hey don't hit him so hard, next time."

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:03.791
and then he started
walking away and he goes,

00:04:03.791 --> 00:04:07.350
"Don't listen to me," he goes,
"you do you keep doing that."

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:08.200
- Interesting.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.124
- You remember, it was Brandon
Lunik that told us that.

00:04:11.124 --> 00:04:12.380
- [Gary] Interesting.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:16.240
- Yeah, I mean, as they get
older and start to realize,

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.850
okay it's not cool to have
girls on your team or--

00:04:18.850 --> 00:04:21.000
- [Gary] Yeah, the dynamic
changed a little bit.

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:22.860
- The dynamic, yeah it
changed quite a bit,

00:04:22.860 --> 00:04:27.170
and so some of the things
guys, very not appropriate for.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:28.420
- Yeah, especially in today's environment.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:29.810
- Yeah, very not appropriate,

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:31.267
but some of the things they would say,

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:33.970
"Oh, the girls are the
best players on your team."

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:36.000
Well, our team's beating yours, so.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.317
- I see, so got it.

00:04:37.317 --> 00:04:39.685
There was from the opposing team,

00:04:39.685 --> 00:04:40.620
they would kind of like talk about that.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.220
- Yeah so I mean you're saying that yet,

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:45.620
I mean, just not the smartest.

00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.370
- And how did you guys think
about it, obviously thinkin'

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.399
about your age, at that
point where were we,

00:04:51.399 --> 00:04:52.870
educate me here a little bit,

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.460
where were we with Olympic female hockey?

00:04:55.460 --> 00:04:59.550
- So, the first Olympics
for women's hockey was '98.

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:02.018
- [Gary] '98, so how old were?

00:05:02.018 --> 00:05:03.690
- We were eight, we were in first grade.

00:05:03.690 --> 00:05:05.228
- Were you fucking pumped?

00:05:05.228 --> 00:05:06.061
- [Sisters] Yeah.

00:05:06.061 --> 00:05:07.070
- Did that hit your radar?

00:05:07.070 --> 00:05:08.410
Were you like, wait a minute?

00:05:08.410 --> 00:05:09.243
- [Sisters] Yeah.

00:05:09.243 --> 00:05:10.597
- Yeah, we played a bunch
of sports, growing up,

00:05:10.597 --> 00:05:12.530
and our mom was a huge Olympics fan,

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:15.070
so whenever the Olympics
were on, we were watching,

00:05:15.070 --> 00:05:16.700
not just hockey, we were just watching--

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:17.670
- Was your mom one of those people

00:05:17.670 --> 00:05:19.250
that loved the back story and,

00:05:19.250 --> 00:05:20.830
- Yep, loved the stories.
- Yep, oh yeah.

00:05:20.830 --> 00:05:23.170
- Costas would get her
so into the farm girl

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:25.990
and now her whole life
predicated on 8:18 p.m.,

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:28.900
to watch the rings, to make
sure that little farm girl won.

00:05:28.900 --> 00:05:29.733
- [Sisters] Yep.

00:05:29.733 --> 00:05:31.220
- Classic Olympic marketing.

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:35.290
- Yeah, our mom just
loves it all so we just,

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:37.125
whatever sport we were
playing at the time,

00:05:37.125 --> 00:05:38.760
'cause we play a ton
of sports, growing up,

00:05:38.760 --> 00:05:39.593
it was, we're gonna go--

00:05:39.593 --> 00:05:41.180
- What was your favorite sport growing up,

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.120
actually on that note?

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:43.420
What you liked the most,

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.400
not necessarily what you were best at.

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:48.660
- Aside from hockey, I
would say soccer and--

00:05:48.660 --> 00:05:50.280
- But hockey was number one?

00:05:50.280 --> 00:05:52.012
It was such a religion in your family.

00:05:52.012 --> 00:05:52.970
Was that what it was?

00:05:52.970 --> 00:05:55.447
- If it wasn't the wintertime
though, we love like soccer.

00:05:55.447 --> 00:05:59.660
- But I mean pure joy,
out of you, actually,

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:02.710
and you're, call it six to 12 years old,

00:06:02.710 --> 00:06:05.330
if the world said you
can only play one thing,

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:07.680
not where your opportunities are,

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:10.090
what do you enjoy the most, was it hockey?

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:11.720
- I would say was hockey and soccer.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:12.553
- Yeah.

00:06:12.553 --> 00:06:13.626
Hockey and soccer.

00:06:13.626 --> 00:06:14.890
We loved it.
- Well, in North Dakota,

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:15.870
I don't know how it is New York,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:17.400
but in North Dakota you can play--

00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:19.070
- It's definitely very
different than North Dakota,

00:06:19.070 --> 00:06:20.190
from New York, I promise you.

00:06:20.190 --> 00:06:22.390
I don't know where you're
going, but it's different.

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:24.620
- You can play high school varsity sports

00:06:24.620 --> 00:06:28.267
as a seventh and eight grader
so we played varsity soccer

00:06:28.267 --> 00:06:30.819
and ran cross-country on varsity

00:06:30.819 --> 00:06:32.470
as seventh and eight graders

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:35.100
and so we were as 12 and 13 year olds,

00:06:35.100 --> 00:06:37.410
we were playing with juniors and seniors

00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:40.120
in high school in soccer and we loved it

00:06:40.120 --> 00:06:42.920
and we did really well
so I would say soccer

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:45.140
if it wasn't hockey for sure.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:47.640
- And so let's keep going
through the progression.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:48.810
You go to boarding school.

00:06:48.810 --> 00:06:49.643
- [Jocelyn] Yep.

00:06:49.643 --> 00:06:50.980
- Where'd you guys,
you guys went together?

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:51.840
- [Jocelyn] Yes.

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:54.744
- Was there ever a consideration
to not be together?

00:06:54.744 --> 00:06:56.190
Did you guys go through that phase?

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.404
- Because we left home at
that age, our parents--

00:06:58.404 --> 00:06:59.250
- [Gary] How old?

00:06:59.250 --> 00:07:01.180
- We would have been 15.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.960
They only went that early
because we were together.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:05.270
- [Gary] It made them feel comfortable.

00:07:05.270 --> 00:07:06.490
- Yeah, it was definitely I think

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:07.860
a comfort thing for our parents,

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:10.210
but we went to Shattuck-St. Mary's,

00:07:10.210 --> 00:07:13.350
which is a pretty
prestigious hockey school.

00:07:13.350 --> 00:07:15.740
- [Gary] Top five kind of school?

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:16.573
- Probably, top.

00:07:16.573 --> 00:07:19.830
- Sydney Crosby, Nathan
MacKinnon, Zach Parise.

00:07:19.830 --> 00:07:22.513
You could name umpteen NHL guys.

00:07:23.422 --> 00:07:27.020
And us two and then Brianna
Decker, Amanda Kessel,

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:29.420
we we're all on the
last two Olympic teams,

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:31.270
three for us, but they've produced

00:07:31.270 --> 00:07:33.000
a number of pretty good hockey players.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:33.960
- So, huge program.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:34.793
- [Sisters] Yes.

00:07:34.793 --> 00:07:35.770
- Women's team.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:36.603
- [Sisters] Yes.

00:07:36.603 --> 00:07:38.043
- And were you guys good?

00:07:38.043 --> 00:07:39.767
I mean, I know you guys were good.

00:07:39.767 --> 00:07:40.820
Did the team?

00:07:40.820 --> 00:07:43.240
- Yeah, we won three out of
four national championships,

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:44.250
the four years were there.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:45.604
- [Gary] I would say that's good.

00:07:45.604 --> 00:07:46.590
- It's pretty good.

00:07:46.590 --> 00:07:48.180
- So let's really focus
in on the one you lost.

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:49.340
- [Monique] We're still
mad about the one--

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:50.800
- [Jocelyn] Yeah, I know.

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:52.170
- I'm being dead serious.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:54.580
I love losing, it drives
the shit out of me,

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:55.930
so I'm actually very curious.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:58.510
What year did, so these were
your four high school years,

00:07:58.510 --> 00:07:59.343
in theory, so which year did you not win?

00:07:59.343 --> 00:08:01.030
- [Sisters] It was our senior year.

00:08:01.030 --> 00:08:02.010
- Fuck you.

00:08:02.010 --> 00:08:02.970
- Yeah.
- I know.

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:04.414
- No.

00:08:04.414 --> 00:08:05.422
You were completely convinced,

00:08:05.422 --> 00:08:06.870
you're like wow, we're
gonna win all, right?

00:08:06.870 --> 00:08:09.406
- We out shot the team 42 to 11,

00:08:09.406 --> 00:08:10.950
in the quarter finals.
- The goalie stood

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:11.783
on her head?

00:08:11.783 --> 00:08:13.130
- Yeah, it was probably the best game

00:08:13.130 --> 00:08:14.210
she's ever played in her entire career.

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:15.690
- Do you hate her deeply?

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:17.495
- I don't know who she is but yes.

00:08:17.495 --> 00:08:19.034
- Yep, still mad about it.

00:08:19.034 --> 00:08:20.741
- So wait, in the quarter finals?

00:08:20.741 --> 00:08:21.632
- [Jocelyn] Yeah.

00:08:21.632 --> 00:08:23.513
- Was the whole place stunned?

00:08:23.513 --> 00:08:24.879
- [Sisters] Yeah.

00:08:24.879 --> 00:08:26.211
- You guys were three-time defending.

00:08:26.211 --> 00:08:27.635
- Still stunned, still stunned.

00:08:27.635 --> 00:08:28.880
- Yeah, we're still mad about it.

00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:31.660
- Is that your most devastating loss?

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:34.230
- Oh, Sochi, gold medal game.
- No, Sochi.

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:35.531
- Olympics, still--

00:08:35.531 --> 00:08:36.420
- [Gary] Kills you.

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:37.920
- Still mad about that one.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:41.100
So background around that,
we were up two to nothing,

00:08:41.100 --> 00:08:42.798
with about three minutes left in the game.

00:08:42.798 --> 00:08:44.569
- [Gary] I remember
these highlights, fuck.

00:08:44.569 --> 00:08:45.661
- It's comin' back to you?

00:08:45.661 --> 00:08:46.494
It's comin' back to me.

00:08:46.494 --> 00:08:47.420
- [Gary] It came back real hard.

00:08:47.420 --> 00:08:50.680
- Yeah, they came back, tied
it up with a minute left.

00:08:50.680 --> 00:08:52.950
We go into overtime, lose in overtime.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:56.234
So that was, even though we won in 2018--

00:08:56.234 --> 00:09:00.901
- How often will you think
about that game in real life?

00:09:00.901 --> 00:09:05.901
Do you think about it once a
year, once a day, once a month?

00:09:06.100 --> 00:09:08.903
- I think, well now that we're parents,

00:09:08.903 --> 00:09:10.808
it's not as I would say...

00:09:10.808 --> 00:09:12.950
- [Gary] You're able
to think about it less?

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:13.783
- Yeah.

00:09:13.783 --> 00:09:14.616
- [Gary] Yeah, fair enough.

00:09:14.616 --> 00:09:17.231
'Cause you're busy, you're
parenting, it's perspective.

00:09:17.231 --> 00:09:20.100
But I would say when we think about

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:21.400
our hockey career, there's always--

00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:22.600
- [Gary] Well that, of course.

00:09:22.600 --> 00:09:24.200
- Yeah, you say you hate losing

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:25.717
and it's you hate losing
more than you love to win.

00:09:25.717 --> 00:09:27.300
- But I'm talking more like,
back to parent life, right?

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:29.440
You're driving to the supermarket

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:31.993
and for some reason, you think about,

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.557
fuck, what if I just back checked that.

00:09:36.557 --> 00:09:38.580
I just think about, I
think about this shit

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:41.770
in pick-up basketball that
nobody on earth remembers,

00:09:41.770 --> 00:09:44.200
so I'm like man, real
fucking greats at their...

00:09:45.068 --> 00:09:47.220
For everybody who's listening
that has any context

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:49.530
for hockey that that's an
unusual thing to happen

00:09:49.530 --> 00:09:52.460
in a hockey match,
especially at that magnitude.

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.860
And you start thinking
about, I don't know,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:57.645
I just think about, do
you think about, oh fuck,

00:09:57.645 --> 00:09:59.890
did I hit the post in the second period

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:00.826
and it would've been three, nothing?

00:10:00.826 --> 00:10:01.990
I don't know the nuances of the game,

00:10:01.990 --> 00:10:06.360
of that specific game, but I just wonder,

00:10:06.360 --> 00:10:08.340
especially the way you framed it up.

00:10:08.340 --> 00:10:10.750
When you grew up in a
family that is framed up

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:12.810
the way you guys just framed it up,

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:15.650
I don't think people, you know it's funny,

00:10:15.650 --> 00:10:18.650
I associate with athletes a lot
because of entrepreneurship.

00:10:19.654 --> 00:10:21.645
I'm a very unique entrepreneur

00:10:21.645 --> 00:10:23.909
and the fact that entrepreneurship,

00:10:23.909 --> 00:10:25.140
you know what's funny?

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:26.730
I actually associate with things

00:10:26.730 --> 00:10:28.020
like women's hockey quite a bit.

00:10:28.020 --> 00:10:28.940
Let me explain.

00:10:28.940 --> 00:10:32.410
In the '80s and '90s,
entrepreneurship was not a thing.

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:35.280
This was the thing, it was called school,

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:36.357
and you had to go to
a good college to be--

00:10:36.357 --> 00:10:37.190
- [Monique] Is that your report card?

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:39.000
- This is my horrible report card.

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:40.810
So, in the 80, you know, right now,

00:10:40.810 --> 00:10:42.050
I'm living a very interesting life,

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:43.250
where entrepreneurship is cool

00:10:43.250 --> 00:10:44.870
and everybody wants to be one,

00:10:44.870 --> 00:10:47.610
but when I was really
doing it and sacrificing,

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:49.460
I mean, that's a bad report card.

00:10:49.460 --> 00:10:52.740
You know, when I was doing that,

00:10:52.740 --> 00:10:54.860
I was a purebred entrepreneur,

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:57.560
as similar as I could get to somebody

00:10:57.560 --> 00:10:59.110
who would go away to boarding school

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:00.720
to be there for hockey
and obviously you have to

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:02.240
keep up your grades to be on the team.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:06.060
All that stuff, but it was underrated.

00:11:06.060 --> 00:11:08.870
People thought I was a
loser, even though I was

00:11:08.870 --> 00:11:11.220
making all this money
selling big sports cards,

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:13.100
on the weekend, but
because I got Ds and Fs,

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:14.410
my friend's parents thought I would

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:15.810
never amount to anything.

00:11:15.810 --> 00:11:18.220
It was that much of the religion.

00:11:18.220 --> 00:11:20.820
Anyway, just knowing how I
grew up where everything,

00:11:20.820 --> 00:11:22.290
and it wasn't less about my parents,

00:11:22.290 --> 00:11:24.540
'cause my dad was just
working all the time,

00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:28.563
but it was more like, for
me, it was always in the air.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:34.800
Back to the, I'm imagining
pictures in my head,

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:39.110
of you guys eating peas and
trying to score on each other

00:11:39.110 --> 00:11:41.080
with your brothers with the fork.

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:43.660
It's just in the DNA.

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:46.920
- Yeah, when you think of elite athletes

00:11:46.920 --> 00:11:49.680
and people that are at
the tip of the spear

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:53.330
at whatever they're doing you
look at youth sports today

00:11:53.330 --> 00:11:55.580
and everyone's like oh, I'm gonna go to,

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:57.334
they specialize early,

00:11:57.334 --> 00:11:59.110
they put their kids in all these things,

00:11:59.110 --> 00:12:00.970
but then when you look at the tip of spear

00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:02.590
of who's been successful,

00:12:02.590 --> 00:12:05.010
they're willing to do
what no one else is doing,

00:12:05.010 --> 00:12:07.110
when they get when they get older,

00:12:07.110 --> 00:12:08.600
when it's appropriate to be specializing,

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.350
and everyone thinks they want to do it,

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:12.100
but then when you get
down to the day-to-day,

00:12:12.100 --> 00:12:14.440
of what it takes to get to that level.

00:12:14.440 --> 00:12:16.210
- [Gary] Sacrificing
everything, social life.

00:12:16.210 --> 00:12:19.780
- People aren't actually
willing or wanting to do that.

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:21.670
- [Gary] I couldn't agree more.

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:24.560
- Even when you get to the top of the top,

00:12:24.560 --> 00:12:26.820
there's still people
not willing to do that.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.690
- Yeah, at the top of the top, again,

00:12:29.690 --> 00:12:31.570
you guys have better framework than I do,

00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:32.910
but I'm so passionate about it.

00:12:32.910 --> 00:12:35.280
I look at it very carefully.

00:12:35.280 --> 00:12:36.220
My brother and I also have

00:12:36.220 --> 00:12:39.283
an NFL sports representation
business where we have 25 guys.

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:41.410
I'm sure you saw this.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:43.440
I don't know how you guys
equate for yourselves,

00:12:43.440 --> 00:12:46.080
how hard you worked or
what sacrifices you made,

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:49.690
but at the top of the top
when you see that passiveness,

00:12:49.690 --> 00:12:53.248
you have to marvel at the
extreme talent, right?

00:12:53.248 --> 00:12:56.440
I'm sure you've seen people who've just

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:58.450
not put in the work that
have made the Olympic team,

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:00.200
or your competitors,
and you're like, fuck,

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:02.900
she is just so fucking talented.

00:13:02.900 --> 00:13:05.130
Now, a lot of those players tend to leave

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:06.980
so much on the table
and they could have been

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:11.530
a top 10 player all-time
versus a non all-star.

00:13:11.530 --> 00:13:12.860
That's where the variable comes in.

00:13:12.860 --> 00:13:14.560
- Or they had it being
a one-time Olympian,

00:13:14.560 --> 00:13:16.550
versus a two or three time Olympian.

00:13:16.550 --> 00:13:19.130
- Correct, but I think,
probably from your perspective,

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:21.290
you still marvel at the sheer talent

00:13:21.290 --> 00:13:24.010
to even get to the top
of the top without--

00:13:24.010 --> 00:13:26.510
- Yeah, I mean, we were in New
York, was it two years ago?

00:13:26.510 --> 00:13:29.480
For, it was the year out
event for Pyeongchang

00:13:29.480 --> 00:13:30.687
and we went to, was it the Knicks game?

00:13:30.687 --> 00:13:33.500
- It was our first NBA
game it was actually

00:13:33.500 --> 00:13:35.710
the game that Charles Oakley--

00:13:35.710 --> 00:13:37.230
- [Gary] Got dragged out
of Madison Square Garden.

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:41.720
- Yeah, but we were a
couple rows off the floor

00:13:42.560 --> 00:13:46.474
and we enjoy sports, but
not huge NBA fans, but--

00:13:46.474 --> 00:13:48.884
- [Gary] Understood, the speed, the speed.

00:13:48.884 --> 00:13:52.150
- The speed, and you just
don't appreciate it on TV,

00:13:52.150 --> 00:13:56.300
but when you're on the floor,
like 30 feet away from them.

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:59.110
- It's actually my favorite
thing about hockey,

00:13:59.110 --> 00:14:01.880
when I've taken people from
other countries to a game,

00:14:01.880 --> 00:14:04.840
or when I was a kid,
just the amount of times

00:14:04.840 --> 00:14:07.040
that I've serendipitously through business

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:11.960
been at a hockey game where
somebody rarely watches hockey

00:14:11.960 --> 00:14:16.960
has also skated at the rink for Christmas

00:14:17.590 --> 00:14:20.690
and within the first five
seconds just completely

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:22.890
gets flabbergasted at the skill level

00:14:22.890 --> 00:14:25.150
of the skating needed.

00:14:25.150 --> 00:14:27.800
It's similar to the speed
it takes for somebody

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:29.020
to actually be in the NBA.

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:32.720
- Yeah, it's impressive and those--

00:14:32.720 --> 00:14:33.553
- They're huge.

00:14:33.553 --> 00:14:36.650
- They're so big and the
little space that they have

00:14:36.650 --> 00:14:41.200
to work in, with, we were impressed.

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:42.510
- What are you guys about now?

00:14:42.510 --> 00:14:44.450
What is what is your passion right now?

00:14:44.450 --> 00:14:46.697
Obviously we have, in two years,

00:14:46.697 --> 00:14:48.780
this video will be watched
hundreds and hundreds

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:51.320
and hundreds and hundreds
of thousands of times.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:53.080
What are you guys passionate about?

00:14:53.080 --> 00:14:54.340
What are you trying to put into the world?

00:14:54.340 --> 00:14:57.121
- So we just actually launched
our foundation last night,

00:14:57.121 --> 00:14:58.362
so we are celebrating.

00:14:58.362 --> 00:14:59.195
- Okay, good timing.

00:14:59.195 --> 00:15:00.200
That was a layup question.

00:15:00.200 --> 00:15:01.394
- [Sisters] Yeah.

00:15:01.394 --> 00:15:02.227
(laughing)

00:15:02.227 --> 00:15:03.213
- Teed it up, nice.

00:15:04.136 --> 00:15:06.710
We're really excited, so
we just launched the--

00:15:06.710 --> 00:15:07.543
- [Gary] What's it
called, what it's about?

00:15:07.543 --> 00:15:09.390
- Monique and Jocelyne
Lamoureux Foundation

00:15:09.390 --> 00:15:11.320
and we've been very fortunate to travel

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:14.140
the country with Comcast as ambassadors

00:15:14.140 --> 00:15:16.300
for the corporate values initiatives.

00:15:16.300 --> 00:15:19.650
So we're using our platform
as Olympic athletes

00:15:19.650 --> 00:15:23.250
to give back and that's been amazing to do

00:15:23.250 --> 00:15:25.360
on a national level and to travel,

00:15:25.360 --> 00:15:27.520
but now we basically asked ourselves

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:29.360
what can we do on a day to day?

00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:33.700
What are we really doing
in our own community?

00:15:33.700 --> 00:15:35.560
So we felt like launching a foundation

00:15:35.560 --> 00:15:39.700
was the way to do that,
so we're gonna give back.

00:15:39.700 --> 00:15:43.750
Our first initiative is
we're going to announce soon,

00:15:43.750 --> 00:15:46.497
but probably will be with
the school lunch program.

00:15:46.497 --> 00:15:47.372
Yeah.

00:15:47.372 --> 00:15:49.490
- Yeah, and our credo is
cheer for the one behind,

00:15:49.490 --> 00:15:51.750
so our mom when we were younger,

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:53.450
all the different sports we do,

00:15:53.450 --> 00:15:55.220
if it was ever something
that we were competing

00:15:55.220 --> 00:15:57.780
against each other,
swimming or cross-country

00:15:57.780 --> 00:15:59.890
or whatever it was, it
was, I'm gonna cheer

00:15:59.890 --> 00:16:01.510
for the one that's behind,
because she didn't want

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:04.062
to have to pick one over the other.

00:16:04.062 --> 00:16:06.040
- First of all, I'm
already all about your mom,

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:09.509
because I'm also, by default,
more underdog nature.

00:16:09.509 --> 00:16:11.750
I've watched a lot, I actually was,

00:16:11.750 --> 00:16:12.860
it's funny you guys went there.

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:15.240
I was about to ask you
a question about this

00:16:15.240 --> 00:16:17.830
and it's been funny, I
noticed, the same reason

00:16:17.830 --> 00:16:20.530
everybody in New York is
wearing Steph Curry jerseys,

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:22.580
because people are bandwagon fans,

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:24.810
because they don't have
their own self-esteem,

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:26.720
and I've watched a lot of parents

00:16:26.720 --> 00:16:29.550
actually cheer for the kid that's better

00:16:29.550 --> 00:16:32.560
and I've always been fascinated
by that because I love this

00:16:32.560 --> 00:16:35.366
cheer for the one, that would
absolutely be the way for me,

00:16:35.366 --> 00:16:38.660
so I'm a huge fan of your mom.

00:16:38.660 --> 00:16:40.258
- She's great, she makes a good latte too.

00:16:40.258 --> 00:16:41.091
- Yeah, she does.

00:16:41.091 --> 00:16:41.924
- [Gary] Really?

00:16:41.924 --> 00:16:42.757
- Linda's lattes, yeah.

00:16:42.757 --> 00:16:43.920
- [Gary] Is that a thing or you guys?

00:16:43.920 --> 00:16:45.331
- Oh, no, we just tell her.
- Yeah, India knows.

00:16:45.331 --> 00:16:46.347
- Oh, have you had one?

00:16:46.347 --> 00:16:47.889
- [Gary] India, you know
about Linda's lattes?

00:16:47.889 --> 00:16:49.450
- [India] Yeah, their mom gave
me a latte, they're really--

00:16:49.450 --> 00:16:52.300
- India, why don't we
open one in Portland?

00:16:52.300 --> 00:16:53.133
- [India] Great, I love it.

00:16:53.133 --> 00:16:55.560
- Me, you, and the mom team up,

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:56.683
three-headed monster.

00:16:56.683 --> 00:16:57.750
(laughing)

00:16:57.750 --> 00:16:59.850
I'm telling you I know how to, I can help.

00:16:59.850 --> 00:17:01.081
- [India] I know.

00:17:01.081 --> 00:17:02.460
(laughing)

00:17:02.460 --> 00:17:03.293
- Okay.

00:17:03.293 --> 00:17:06.140
- Yeah, so we, that's
kind of something that,

00:17:06.140 --> 00:17:08.310
for us it kind of transcended sports

00:17:08.310 --> 00:17:10.060
and just into other parts of our lives.

00:17:10.060 --> 00:17:13.407
I remember, at the time I
didn't think anything of it,

00:17:13.407 --> 00:17:16.100
but in sixth grade,
there was a kid that sat

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:17.970
next to me, we sat alphabetical order.

00:17:17.970 --> 00:17:19.920
He had Down syndrome, his name was Justin

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:22.827
and he would sit by himself
at lunch I was like,

00:17:22.827 --> 00:17:25.160
"Hey Justin, why don't
you come sit with us?"

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:29.690
So at a table of 10 girls,
he'd come and sit with us

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:32.100
and he kind of became the cool kid.

00:17:32.100 --> 00:17:33.720
So that was in sixth
grade, by eighth grade

00:17:33.720 --> 00:17:35.586
he would just migrate from table to table.

00:17:35.586 --> 00:17:36.419
- [Gary] Love you.

00:17:36.419 --> 00:17:40.178
- And then unbeknownst
to us, I think his mom?

00:17:40.178 --> 00:17:43.220
I think his mom or grandma
sends a letter to the school

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:45.015
and then they sent it to our parents.

00:17:45.015 --> 00:17:47.010
I didn't tell my parents
about it, it was just.

00:17:47.010 --> 00:17:47.910
- [Gary] Yeah, it's life.

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:50.140
- Just being nice.

00:17:50.140 --> 00:17:51.800
So then actually we were doing an event

00:17:51.800 --> 00:17:55.230
a couple months ago and his
grandma can't come to the event

00:17:55.230 --> 00:17:58.260
and then we're doing a quick
meet-and-greet afterwards

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:01.470
and she was like, "I'm Justin's Gram."

00:18:01.470 --> 00:18:03.170
I'm like, "Oh my God,
it's Justin's Grandma."

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:05.330
So it was just little things like that

00:18:05.330 --> 00:18:07.550
where we're just trying to be nice

00:18:07.550 --> 00:18:09.300
and just be a good person.

00:18:09.300 --> 00:18:11.550
- Kindness is so underrated.

00:18:11.550 --> 00:18:12.700
- [Monique] Yes, it is.

00:18:14.450 --> 00:18:17.880
- I push it so heavily,
I'm fascinated by people

00:18:17.880 --> 00:18:20.700
not understanding how life actually works.

00:18:20.700 --> 00:18:22.300
- Well it just doesn't make, like what?

00:18:22.300 --> 00:18:23.716
- [Gary] It makes no practical sense.

00:18:23.716 --> 00:18:26.213
- Why go through life that and be a dink?

00:18:26.213 --> 00:18:28.090
- Well why, because,
unlike the three of us,

00:18:28.090 --> 00:18:30.160
we were fortunate enough
to have a situation

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:33.620
where we weren't hurting inside
and it wasn't manifesting.

00:18:33.620 --> 00:18:36.710
When I watch people not be kind,

00:18:36.710 --> 00:18:39.470
I deploy empathy and
sadness for that person

00:18:39.470 --> 00:18:41.840
because I'm like what's
really going on back home,

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:43.110
is super not interesting.

00:18:43.110 --> 00:18:43.943
- [Jocelyne] Mm hm.

00:18:43.943 --> 00:18:46.250
- That's the manifestation
of a bad environment

00:18:46.250 --> 00:18:49.350
or bad parenting or a
terrible circumstance.

00:18:49.350 --> 00:18:50.183
- [Jocelyne] Mm hm.

00:18:50.183 --> 00:18:51.016
- You know?

00:18:52.220 --> 00:18:54.950
Nonetheless, what is the biggest fight

00:18:54.950 --> 00:18:56.100
you two ever got into?

00:18:56.100 --> 00:18:57.050
Go.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:18:58.260
- Against each other?

00:18:58.260 --> 00:18:59.093
- [Gary] Against each other.

00:18:59.093 --> 00:18:59.926
- Oh, against each other?

00:18:59.926 --> 00:19:00.759
- Well, you know what?

00:19:00.759 --> 00:19:01.592
I'll share this.

00:19:01.592 --> 00:19:02.930
I had braces

00:19:02.930 --> 00:19:04.320
and I also--
- and you didn't?

00:19:04.320 --> 00:19:06.120
- No, I probably should have though.

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:09.150
- I also had to wear
headgear when I was at home.

00:19:09.150 --> 00:19:11.493
I didn't have to wear
it in public at least.

00:19:11.493 --> 00:19:12.700
- Why'd you have to wear,
- We would make fun of her.

00:19:12.700 --> 00:19:14.320
- Oh, the headgear for
the braces, understood.

00:19:14.320 --> 00:19:16.279
- We would make fun of her
- Like some old 1980s shit

00:19:16.279 --> 00:19:17.112
- and go like this.

00:19:17.112 --> 00:19:17.945
- Yeah, all my brothers
would make fun of me.

00:19:17.945 --> 00:19:18.970
- [Gary] Just call you this?

00:19:18.970 --> 00:19:19.803
- Yeah.

00:19:19.803 --> 00:19:21.080
- Love.

00:19:21.080 --> 00:19:22.930
You guys were so unstoppable.

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:25.330
I always think about the
reason I'm unstoppable

00:19:25.330 --> 00:19:28.010
emotionally is all the bad
stuff happened so early,

00:19:28.010 --> 00:19:30.490
like didn't speak English,
I was an immigrant,

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:32.260
got picked on, terrible in school,

00:19:32.260 --> 00:19:33.630
everybody thinks I'm a loser,

00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:35.150
wasn't athletically inclined enough,

00:19:35.150 --> 00:19:37.940
that very quickly my hand-eye
coordination skills went away

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:39.250
by third grade and I'm losing,

00:19:39.250 --> 00:19:41.160
