00:39:28.943 --> 00:39:30.930
then even though the outcome
might not be what you wanted

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:33.650
it to be, you can be
satisfied with your effort

00:39:33.650 --> 00:39:35.230
and you can't be disappointed in yourself,

00:39:35.230 --> 00:39:37.820
and I think by having that mindset,

00:39:37.820 --> 00:39:40.650
I think that helps create confidence.

00:39:40.650 --> 00:39:42.620
As far as the twin question goes,

00:39:42.620 --> 00:39:44.850
our parents always put
everything in us together.

00:39:44.850 --> 00:39:46.550
And we just always did everything together

00:39:46.550 --> 00:39:49.700
and our mom always said,
"Two is better than one."

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:51.670
So we were always unique in that aspect,

00:39:51.670 --> 00:39:53.490
that we have the same group of friends,

00:39:53.490 --> 00:39:56.170
we did all the same sports and whatnot,

00:39:56.170 --> 00:39:57.592
and some twins don't get along like that

00:39:57.592 --> 00:39:58.425
(kids yelling)

00:39:58.425 --> 00:39:59.857
or spend a lot of time together--

00:40:00.700 --> 00:40:01.627
- [Gary] I think the girls

00:40:01.627 --> 00:40:02.460
like that answer.
- So I think just letting.

00:40:02.460 --> 00:40:04.660
Yeah, I think just letting
that develop over time,

00:40:04.660 --> 00:40:07.020
'cause they might end up
having all the same interests,

00:40:07.020 --> 00:40:09.640
they might not and as they get older

00:40:09.640 --> 00:40:11.390
and their personalities develop,

00:40:11.390 --> 00:40:14.343
I think that you'll kind
of find your way with that.

00:40:15.360 --> 00:40:18.060
- [Ryan] Awesome, thank you very much.

00:40:18.060 --> 00:40:19.330
- Yeah, just add my two cents,

00:40:19.330 --> 00:40:20.640
'cause I'm super passionate about,

00:40:20.640 --> 00:40:22.240
obviously, I really can't speak

00:40:22.240 --> 00:40:24.423
with any authority on the twin level,

00:40:24.423 --> 00:40:26.420
(laughing)

00:40:26.420 --> 00:40:28.210
but there are two things
that stand out to me.

00:40:28.210 --> 00:40:30.990
Number one, I do think reverse engineering

00:40:30.990 --> 00:40:33.660
individuals at scale is something
I'm very passionate about.

00:40:33.660 --> 00:40:34.910
Having all these different employees

00:40:34.910 --> 00:40:37.300
and there's so many different
dynamics and I think it is

00:40:37.300 --> 00:40:39.040
a fine line between the
two is better than one,

00:40:39.040 --> 00:40:42.480
which I think, the
optimism that that breeds,

00:40:42.480 --> 00:40:44.330
I think is incredible and I think is,

00:40:45.500 --> 00:40:47.576
it's always better to lean into optimism

00:40:47.576 --> 00:40:49.840
versus pessimism and cynicism,

00:40:49.840 --> 00:40:52.610
but where I'm about to go with
the rest of that sentence,

00:40:52.610 --> 00:40:54.450
is actually the answer to confidence.

00:40:54.450 --> 00:40:59.020
I think the number one vulnerability
in society is delusion.

00:40:59.020 --> 00:41:00.570
I think that parents are creating

00:41:00.570 --> 00:41:03.710
a remarkable level of delusion
over the last 20 years,

00:41:03.710 --> 00:41:06.950
because parents have
become so micro-managing

00:41:06.950 --> 00:41:10.280
of their kids' feelings and
actually know their day-to-days

00:41:10.280 --> 00:41:14.850
too much and are trying to
protect them from the world,

00:41:14.850 --> 00:41:17.670
which unfortunately is
fundamentally impossible

00:41:17.670 --> 00:41:20.410
and I believe that we have
one to two full generations,

00:41:20.410 --> 00:41:23.100
right now, that are closer to zoo animals

00:41:23.100 --> 00:41:24.400
than they are humans.

00:41:24.400 --> 00:41:27.400
And so as I was listening to the stories,

00:41:27.400 --> 00:41:28.690
as we were talking here,

00:41:28.690 --> 00:41:32.210
I think one of the great
things that is true about

00:41:32.210 --> 00:41:35.350
the three of us' upbringing
was we were in frameworks,

00:41:35.350 --> 00:41:39.410
because of circumstances,
where losing wasn't demonized

00:41:39.410 --> 00:41:42.800
or protected against, it was
the merit of the outcome,

00:41:42.800 --> 00:41:45.530
which got us comfortable with the idea of,

00:41:45.530 --> 00:41:48.210
which meant by the time
we were actually 22,

00:41:48.210 --> 00:41:50.500
we actually knew that that
was part of the variable

00:41:50.500 --> 00:41:53.820
and it is remarkably stunning to me

00:41:53.820 --> 00:41:55.970
to watch the enormity of 22 year olds

00:41:55.970 --> 00:41:59.930
that go into the world who
demonize and fear losing

00:41:59.930 --> 00:42:03.430
or setbacks, because they've
been in an over-coddled,

00:42:03.430 --> 00:42:05.410
fake framework environment,

00:42:05.410 --> 00:42:08.100
where grades have been the only variable

00:42:08.100 --> 00:42:11.320
or seventh place trophies feel
close to a first-place trophy

00:42:13.129 --> 00:42:17.560
or mommy and daddy pay
for Uber or the apartment

00:42:17.560 --> 00:42:21.560
and we are just in a
very entitled ecosystem.

00:42:21.560 --> 00:42:23.010
I think the biggest thing you can do for

00:42:23.010 --> 00:42:27.200
those three-year-olds, to create
self-esteem and confidence,

00:42:27.200 --> 00:42:32.200
is to make them be in an
environment where losing is reality

00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:36.410
and almost even put on a pedestal,

00:42:36.410 --> 00:42:39.500
without it being something
that they dwell on,

00:42:39.500 --> 00:42:40.723
post the outcome.

00:42:41.680 --> 00:42:43.358
- You have to learn how to
fail and learn how to lose,

00:42:43.358 --> 00:42:44.874
and learn from it.

00:42:44.874 --> 00:42:47.093
- In a real way, not in a fake way.

00:42:47.970 --> 00:42:49.580
The amount of parents
that I'm watching, like,

00:42:49.580 --> 00:42:52.240
Gary, they hear me talking about this,

00:42:52.240 --> 00:42:55.067
and they'll give me the
full 15 sentence email

00:42:55.067 --> 00:42:56.770
and it's like, "I let my kids lose",

00:42:56.770 --> 00:42:59.810
and then it's some convoluted fake loss.

00:42:59.810 --> 00:43:01.290
- [Monique] Yeah.

00:43:01.290 --> 00:43:05.260
- Like, they lost in
color wars, this summer.

00:43:05.260 --> 00:43:09.810
I'm like, bro, cool but let's
really get in underneath this,

00:43:09.810 --> 00:43:12.980
and this gets into,
athletics is fascinating.

00:43:12.980 --> 00:43:16.320
The amount of parents
that are so delusional

00:43:16.320 --> 00:43:18.080
thinking their kid's going to the league.

00:43:18.080 --> 00:43:18.913
(Jocelyne groans)

00:43:18.913 --> 00:43:20.660
Yeah, this is why you're
reacting like this,

00:43:20.660 --> 00:43:22.443
as an actual professional athlete.

00:43:25.460 --> 00:43:28.630
Breaking news, everybody
listening and watching right now,

00:43:28.630 --> 00:43:30.380
your kid's not going to the league.

00:43:31.360 --> 00:43:32.550
- Whatever league that is.

00:43:32.550 --> 00:43:35.270
- Whatever league that
is, because the math is

00:43:35.270 --> 00:43:39.650
uncomfortably against you
and so the amount of parents

00:43:39.650 --> 00:43:42.340
that are wrapping their self-esteem

00:43:42.340 --> 00:43:45.130
into their kids' outcomes in school

00:43:45.130 --> 00:43:50.130
and in sports, this is one
huge game of mass insecurity,

00:43:50.130 --> 00:43:51.400
at the parent level.

00:43:51.400 --> 00:43:53.330
You want those two girls to be happy?

00:43:53.330 --> 00:43:56.820
Never let your mom, siblings, teachers,

00:43:56.820 --> 00:43:58.450
or best friends points-of-view

00:43:58.450 --> 00:44:01.683
on your children ever penetrate your mind.

00:44:02.810 --> 00:44:03.643
- [Ryan] That's awesome.

00:44:03.643 --> 00:44:05.050
Yeah and that's that's one thing that

00:44:05.050 --> 00:44:07.730
I really tried my hardest
at, even with them,

00:44:07.730 --> 00:44:11.260
just he basics of falling,
I instantly say to them,

00:44:11.260 --> 00:44:15.400
hey, that was a fantastic fall, you fell,

00:44:15.400 --> 00:44:17.633
yeah, you gotta scrape, you got a boo-boo,

00:44:18.610 --> 00:44:22.755
that sucks, but let's continue
forward and let's continue--

00:44:22.755 --> 00:44:26.085
- And you need to find
the balance between,

00:44:26.085 --> 00:44:28.990
probably what these two
amazing gals grew up with

00:44:28.990 --> 00:44:30.720
which was, there was no fantastic

00:44:30.720 --> 00:44:32.720
fall words out of their dad's mouth.

00:44:32.720 --> 00:44:34.121
There was get the fuck up, out of their--

00:44:34.121 --> 00:44:35.640
- Yeah or you just get ignored.

00:44:35.640 --> 00:44:38.440
- Yeah, so I do think,
this is, and by the way,

00:44:38.440 --> 00:44:41.460
let me just say this
as I keep on this rant.

00:44:41.460 --> 00:44:42.780
Giving parenting advice is

00:44:42.780 --> 00:44:44.630
the least interesting thing on earth.

00:44:45.770 --> 00:44:47.110
It's just not interesting,

00:44:47.110 --> 00:44:49.570
but it's a fun macro
debate, not a micro debate.

00:44:49.570 --> 00:44:52.020
So, before I go any further, you do you.

00:44:52.020 --> 00:44:55.350
However, my point of view, in the macro,

00:44:55.350 --> 00:45:00.350
is all of us have to decide
what a fantastic boo-boo is,

00:45:00.550 --> 00:45:03.870
versus a, boo-boos are
real, here's a band-aid,

00:45:03.870 --> 00:45:06.560
let's go and everything in between.

00:45:06.560 --> 00:45:07.920
I think we're in a culture right now

00:45:07.920 --> 00:45:12.330
where fantastic boo-boo
is very much more common

00:45:12.330 --> 00:45:15.070
and it's politically incorrect
to do what their dad did

00:45:15.070 --> 00:45:17.700
or what I believe in
of like get the fuck up

00:45:17.700 --> 00:45:19.780
and you ought to know your kid,

00:45:19.780 --> 00:45:22.900
and some kids respond incredibly
well to get the fuck up,

00:45:22.900 --> 00:45:25.890
some don't, you got to
stay in listening mode.

00:45:25.890 --> 00:45:29.143
But this is just real life,
this is just real life.

00:45:30.021 --> 00:45:32.030
I think my mom is the
greatest mother of all time.

00:45:32.030 --> 00:45:35.450
I'm the byproduct of it
and the one time my mom

00:45:35.450 --> 00:45:38.030
tried to enter a conversation
of how I should parent,

00:45:38.030 --> 00:45:41.240
it was a three second conversation
of shut the fuck up mom.

00:45:41.240 --> 00:45:43.730
And I realized when I did that to somebody

00:45:43.730 --> 00:45:47.040
who I think is my hero,
is the best mom on earth,

00:45:47.040 --> 00:45:49.150
I was excited because I was like, wow,

00:45:49.150 --> 00:45:51.050
I really believe in the
shit that I talk about.

00:45:51.050 --> 00:45:53.720
I am not gonna let anybody
penetrate my mindset.

00:45:53.720 --> 00:45:56.780
It's very cute that you
fly in on the weekends,

00:45:56.780 --> 00:45:58.360
once in a while and have a point-of-view.

00:45:58.360 --> 00:46:00.370
You're not here 24, it's the same reason

00:46:00.370 --> 00:46:03.830
that I even walk, tread
lightly with my opinions,

00:46:03.830 --> 00:46:06.490
to my wife who's there
actually every second

00:46:06.490 --> 00:46:09.480
versus me flying in on the weekends.

00:46:09.480 --> 00:46:11.460
It's the one place I'm
like, fuck this sucks,

00:46:11.460 --> 00:46:13.730
for me to stay and not be a hypocrite,

00:46:13.730 --> 00:46:15.173
I got to be thoughtful here.

00:46:16.290 --> 00:46:18.100
That's what I would say bro.

00:46:18.100 --> 00:46:22.400
Kids and everybody needs to feel safe,

00:46:22.400 --> 00:46:27.310
but not entitled and that fucking
fine line is hard as fuck,

00:46:27.310 --> 00:46:29.650
but if you keep repeating that sentence,

00:46:29.650 --> 00:46:31.500
it allows you to have multiple moves.

00:46:31.500 --> 00:46:34.660
The biggest issue that parents
have now is they've one move.

00:46:34.660 --> 00:46:36.870
I think if you go fantastic boo-boo

00:46:36.870 --> 00:46:39.390
and get the fuck up and you mix them up,

00:46:39.390 --> 00:46:42.580
you've got a much better
chance than one or the other.

00:46:42.580 --> 00:46:44.863
- [Ryan] And that's the main reason why I,

00:46:44.863 --> 00:46:45.696
- [Jocelyne] I like that.

00:46:45.696 --> 00:46:46.529
- Right, that was interesting.

00:46:46.529 --> 00:46:47.362
- get up and going.

00:46:47.362 --> 00:46:48.527
- I never said that
before, I like that too.

00:46:48.527 --> 00:46:49.910
- They have to keep up and get going,

00:46:49.910 --> 00:46:51.020
'cause I've seen these
kids, time and time again,

00:46:51.020 --> 00:46:52.230
where they'll sit in one spot

00:46:52.230 --> 00:46:55.460
and just cry for 10 minutes
about this one boo-boo,

00:46:55.460 --> 00:46:57.950
where there's plenty more opportunities

00:46:57.950 --> 00:47:02.600
to go ahead and keep playing
and move forward too.

00:47:02.600 --> 00:47:04.460
- I've already convinced my six-year-old,

00:47:04.460 --> 00:47:07.150
who lives for basketball,
that he will never dunk

00:47:07.150 --> 00:47:11.840
and that took two months of
being okay with him, I mean.

00:47:11.840 --> 00:47:16.770
He's also his dad's son, he cries 24/7.

00:47:16.770 --> 00:47:20.330
All my emotional intelligence,
now is my greatest strength,

00:47:20.330 --> 00:47:24.400
but from six to 15, I couldn't
control feeling everything.

00:47:24.400 --> 00:47:26.690
And so I just fucking cried.

00:47:26.690 --> 00:47:31.690
Every single thing I lost in,
from 1982 to 1989, I cried.

00:47:32.530 --> 00:47:33.420
Pool, cried.

00:47:33.420 --> 00:47:34.260
Football, cried.

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:35.093
Tennis, cried.

00:47:35.093 --> 00:47:37.590
Cried, cried for kids that got picked on.

00:47:37.590 --> 00:47:38.730
That's why I love you so much.

00:47:38.730 --> 00:47:40.850
Cried for kids that got
picked on in school.

00:47:40.850 --> 00:47:42.580
A kid's getting picked on at lunch

00:47:42.580 --> 00:47:43.950
and I'm over here crying for that person.

00:47:43.950 --> 00:47:45.610
(sisters laughing)

00:47:45.610 --> 00:47:49.340
Like couldn't control them, so
my son's got that thing too.

00:47:49.340 --> 00:47:52.780
So telling him he's not gonna dunk now,

00:47:52.780 --> 00:47:54.370
is painful as fuck.

00:47:54.370 --> 00:47:56.770
Most parents don't want micro-pain.

00:47:56.770 --> 00:47:58.630
They don't realize they're
creating macro pain.

00:47:58.630 --> 00:48:02.170
It hurts hell watching
him cry for 20 minutes,

00:48:02.170 --> 00:48:05.120
full steam, of basically, fuck you daddy,

00:48:05.120 --> 00:48:09.160
why can't I dunk, and the
answer's genetics, bro.

00:48:09.160 --> 00:48:12.900
It's just not gonna happen, but he also,

00:48:12.900 --> 00:48:14.360
and this is crazy for me to say,

00:48:14.360 --> 00:48:15.430
I can't even believe this happened,

00:48:15.430 --> 00:48:18.690
he almost actually beat me
in Pop-A-Shot basketball

00:48:18.690 --> 00:48:22.000
this weekend, because his
jump shot is so insane,

00:48:22.000 --> 00:48:24.610
'cause he stopped dunking
on his little fucking thing,

00:48:24.610 --> 00:48:26.150
after I finally got through to him

00:48:26.150 --> 00:48:28.590
and he's shooting from such range

00:48:28.590 --> 00:48:31.960
and his euro-step and
his left hand and now,

00:48:31.960 --> 00:48:35.050
he will be an unbelievably
good pickup basketball player

00:48:35.050 --> 00:48:37.545
one day and that's awesome.

00:48:37.545 --> 00:48:42.545
So to me, that's something
parents need to bring

00:48:42.960 --> 00:48:46.720
into the framework, versus
what I see 90% doing,

00:48:46.720 --> 00:48:48.550
which is the reverse of what I just did,

00:48:48.550 --> 00:48:51.830
which is you'll dunk one
day son, you're gonna dunk,

00:48:51.830 --> 00:48:54.133
and then me paying, because of my ability,

00:48:55.550 --> 00:48:57.520
NBA coaches to teach him,
when he's in eighth grade,

00:48:57.520 --> 00:49:00.170
on one-on-one and then
somewhere around 15,

00:49:00.170 --> 00:49:01.960
he's like I'm fucking delusional.

00:49:01.960 --> 00:49:04.655
- Well, if you just use sports, for us,

00:49:04.655 --> 00:49:06.370
it's a microcosm of life,

00:49:06.370 --> 00:49:09.047
you use it to teach being a good teammate,

00:49:09.047 --> 00:49:12.310
take direction from others,
listen to your coaches,

00:49:12.310 --> 00:49:14.330
having a good attitude, working hard,

00:49:14.330 --> 00:49:16.150
accountability, all
those things transcend--

00:49:16.150 --> 00:49:17.210
- You want to talk about one that,

00:49:17.210 --> 00:49:18.430
I know we're so over and I got a run,

00:49:18.430 --> 00:49:19.867
but you know what my favorite one is

00:49:19.867 --> 00:49:21.700
and I'm sure you've done this.

00:49:21.700 --> 00:49:23.980
You know what my favorite
thing that people learn about

00:49:23.980 --> 00:49:26.500
in sports that nobody ever says out loud?

00:49:26.500 --> 00:49:29.435
Knowing when not to listen to a coach.

00:49:29.435 --> 00:49:33.940
I've never heard anybody say
that and when I watch kids

00:49:33.940 --> 00:49:35.730
or just watching, I'm like (snaps).

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:37.820
Like, I literally watch
that way, like I watch.

00:49:37.820 --> 00:49:39.480
I've hired people here when they've

00:49:39.480 --> 00:49:41.080
been interns, by the way they've played.

00:49:41.080 --> 00:49:43.170
Nate is my partner in Empathy Wines,

00:49:43.170 --> 00:49:45.600
because as an intern the
way he played volleyball

00:49:45.600 --> 00:49:47.740
made me know he would be
good and it played out.

00:49:47.740 --> 00:49:49.840
I'm being serious.

00:49:49.840 --> 00:49:51.810
Knowing when to not listen

00:49:51.810 --> 00:49:53.850
to the coach is such a subtle skill.

00:49:53.850 --> 00:49:54.943
- But be respectful at the same time.

00:49:54.943 --> 00:49:56.157
- [Gary] Correct, well it's funny--

00:49:56.157 --> 00:49:58.200
- And you pretend like you're listening.

00:49:58.200 --> 00:49:59.870
- No, no you nailed it, you know what?

00:49:59.870 --> 00:50:00.870
I think about this.

00:50:00.870 --> 00:50:02.740
I was the most disruptive force

00:50:02.740 --> 00:50:05.290
in my classrooms from
third grade to senior year.

00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:08.000
In our senior breakfast, which
was a really cool tradition

00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:10.567
at our high school,
somebody got up and said,

00:50:10.567 --> 00:50:11.897
"My favorite thing about high school

00:50:11.897 --> 00:50:14.417
"was hoping I was in
Gary Vaynerchuk's class

00:50:14.417 --> 00:50:16.077
"'cause I knew that he would always waste

00:50:16.077 --> 00:50:18.209
"seven to 13 minutes per class."

00:50:18.209 --> 00:50:20.200
(sisters laughing)

00:50:20.200 --> 00:50:22.660
The fact that I was able to do that

00:50:22.660 --> 00:50:26.890
and still be liked by my
teachers is now in hindsight

00:50:26.890 --> 00:50:30.420
so much of what I am,
similar to what you just said

00:50:30.420 --> 00:50:33.210
which is knowing when not
to listen to the coach,

00:50:33.210 --> 00:50:34.890
but not being an issue.

00:50:34.890 --> 00:50:36.760
- Yep, exactly and then you just--

00:50:36.760 --> 00:50:38.810
- 'Cause players on the field still know.

00:50:39.739 --> 00:50:42.614
And that confidence, so anyway,

00:50:42.614 --> 00:50:44.750
nonetheless, thanks for the call.

00:50:44.750 --> 00:50:46.400
Ladies, thank you so
much for being on the,

00:50:46.400 --> 00:50:48.194
so abrupt the way we hung up.

00:50:48.194 --> 00:50:50.420
(laughing)

00:50:50.420 --> 00:50:51.860
I really appreciate.

00:50:51.860 --> 00:50:53.520
This is really fun,
this is how we wrap up,

00:50:53.520 --> 00:50:55.190
you get to ask the question of the day,

00:50:55.190 --> 00:50:58.003
any question you want for
the audience, fire away.

